## 2. Where the cast appears

`std::gir` on the failing program showed, in the graph of `shrink3`:

```
{ state: null, steps: 0 }  DATA   value_type="{ state: Tensor<float32>, steps: int }"
{ state: ..., steps: ... } FILL   value_type="{ state: Tensor<float32>, steps: int }"
...                        JOIN   value_type="{ state: Tensor<float32>, steps: int }"
...                        CAST   value_type="Run"                     origin=38:12
```

So the anonymous literal in the returning arm is an anonymous struct value, the branch joins
the two arms with that anonymous type, and the function's exit converts it to `Run` with an
identity CAST. The reorder form produces no CAST: the front end lowers it to a second FILL that
copies the fields into the declaration's order (H5, `Builder::convertStructValue`). A struct
literal bound with `let r: Run = { ... }` in the declaration's order gets the CAST at the
declaration; one passed to a parameter declared `Run` gets it at the call argument.

## 3. What the exporter now does

`modules/onnx/exporter.cpp`:

- **The CAST body is one function, `Evaluator::evalCast`** (extracted from `evalNode`), so the
  same rules apply wherever a cast is met. Of a constant it is performed now, as before.
- **Identity struct cast: no node.** When both sides are struct types and
  `target->castSafetyFrom(source)` is `Safe` — an anonymous value entering the named type of its
  fields in the declaration's order, which is what the front end builds here — the export-time
  aggregate already holds one value per field in that order. The aggregate is relabelled with the
  target type and no ONNX node is emitted. If the value does not hold one element per field the
  conversion is still refused. A cast whose struct types do not correspond falls through to the
  old refusal (`cast of a model-dependent value to '…'`), with its source position.
- **Reorder cast: not a CAST.** It reaches the exporter as the front end's FILL; `evalFill`
  already builds an aggregate of the target type from the constant base and the computed
  inputs, in the declaration's order, and `emitOutputs` names the outputs after that order
  (`output_state`, `output_steps`). Nothing here had to change; the new test locks it end to end.
- **A CAST at the tail of a loop body is passed through.** `evalLoopBody` had no `Cast` case, so
  the branch under the cast was taken for a value to evaluate (`evalJoin`) instead of for the
  branch that decides whether the recursion goes on. With the identity cast now translating, the
  branch's returning arm (anonymous literal) and its recursive-call arm (the loop's `Run` result)
  met with two type spellings and the export stopped at `the arms of a branch on a model-dependent
  condition yield different types`. `evalLoopBody` now unwraps the cast, asks the branch, and
  applies `evalCast` to the returned value.
- **The branch arms' struct types may differ in spelling.** `collectArmLeaves` required the same
  `camelType` pointer. `sameStructRepresentation` now accepts a named type and the anonymous
  type of its fields in the same order (and only that: two named types remain different types),
  because that pair is exactly what an identity cast joins. The leaves are still compared by
  position and dtype, and the rebuilt arm keeps the first arm's type; the cast above retypes it.

`modules/onnx/passes/export.cpp` (pass route only):

- `constantOf` looks through a struct CAST whose conversion moves nothing before deciding that an
  argument is not a constant. Without this, `std::opt` leaves the CAST of the folded literal in
  the graph, and `onnx::export` refused the capture of `export_onnx.cml` as "a value computed at
  run time". The constant is returned with the cast's declared type, so the exporter binds the
  capture with the named type; `onnx::export` and the operator route then produce the same model.

  Folding the same cast in `std::opt`'s `foldStaticCast` was tried first and reverted: it makes
  `test/cases/modules/onnx/refusals/unended_unrolling.cml` stop terminating (the plan's own
  `typed_constant_ends_unrolling` case times out at 60 s), because the simplifier relies on the
  struct CAST staying in the graph to end the unrolling. The look-through in the export pass
  gets the same result without changing what `std::opt` hands to every other consumer; the
  simplifier defect is left for its owner (section 6).

## 4. Tests added

- `test/cases/modules/onnx/ort_struct_casts.cml` and `.py`, plus one entry in
  `test/plans/feat/modules/onnx.plan.toml`. Five models, exported twice (operator and
  `std::macro std::opt onnx::export`) from an example argument: an identity literal returned as
  `Run`, a reordered literal, a `let r: Run = ...` (identity at a declaration), an anonymous value
  given to a parameter declared `Run` (identity at a call argument, returned next to a number
  computed from it), and a reordered literal on the path where a recursion whose depth depends
  on the model input returns (the conversion inside the ONNX Loop). Every model must
  - pass `onnx.checker`'s full check;
  - hold no `Cast` node (only `walk`'s loop condition legitimately casts, and only there is a
    `Cast` allowed);
  - name its outputs after the declaration's fields (`output_state`, `output_steps`), with the
    counter an int64 scalar;
  - match NumPy within 1e-6 for inputs on which the recursion stops after 0, 1 and the maximum 3
    steps, counters exactly.
- No existing expectation was changed or weakened. The case was deliberately built around a
  pre-existing defect (section 6) instead of asserting it.

